You're missing the point.

Yes, spellcasting is one way of differentiating the classes.

So are weapons--otherwise the classes wouldn't _have_ different weapon
skills to begin with.  So are Quest artifacts, Quests, codes of conduct
(a la knights, samurai, monks), and many other things.

Increasing the distinctions between weapon classes doesn't diminish the
distinctions from spellcasting, and if done reasonably could make the
game a bit more interesting for repeat players.

You're the one thinking too small by refusing to consider the
possibility that other facets of the game could be made as varied as
spellcasting.

